# Caddy configuration of the machine, deployed to /etc/caddy/Caddyfile.
#
# THREE VARIABLES COME FROM THE ENVIRONMENT, placed by bin/deploy-caddy.sh
# in /etc/caddy/sitesolide.env and read by systemd like the Cloudflare token:
#
#   SITESOLIDE_ZONE        the zone served, of which each project gets a subdomain
#   SITESOLIDE_ACME_EMAIL  the address the certificate authority notifies
#   SITESOLIDE_SLUG        the label that carries the slug, {labels.2} for a zone
#                          with two labels, {labels.3} for a zone with three
#
# Caddy substitutes them BEFORE reading the file: a `caddy validate` run without
# them rejects the configuration, and that rejection is not a flaw of the
# Caddyfile.
#
# Placed by bin/deploy-caddy.sh, never by hand: that script backs up, validates
# with the Cloudflare token the way systemd supplies it, reloads, queries the
# sites, and restores the previous version at the slightest misstep.
#
# Principle: a client site is a directory, not a process. The subdomain is
# resolved to /srv/sites/<slug>/public by naming convention, so adding a client
# calls for no modification of this file.

{
	email {$SITESOLIDE_ACME_EMAIL}

	# Certificates for the clients' own domains, obtained on the first
	# request. The "ask" endpoint is not optional: without it, anyone pointing
	# their DNS at this machine would trigger issuances in our name. It answers
	# 200 for the domains present in domaines.map and for those alone; if it is
	# stopped, no issuance takes place.
	on_demand_tls {
		ask http://127.0.0.1:3001/interne/domaine-autorise
	}
}

# TLS policy of the zone served, and of it alone.
#
# The DNS-01 challenge obtains the certificate without exposing a port, and it
# is the only way to obtain a wildcard. It does require, however, a token able
# to modify the zone: the server's one covers only the zone served, so this
# snippet must never be imported by a block serving a client's own domain. Those
# domains go through HTTP-01, see the on-demand block at the end of the file.
#
# Importing this snippet rather than copying the tls block is not only a matter
# of concision: Caddy reuses the wildcard certificate for a subdomain only if
# the block's automation policy is identical to that of the wildcard block. A
# client block that diverged here would obtain its own certificate without
# anything signalling it.
(tls-zone) {
	tls {
		dns cloudflare {env.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN}
	}
}

# Settings shared by every site.
(commun) {
	encode zstd gzip

	header {
		X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
		Referrer-Policy strict-origin-when-cross-origin
	}

	# Cache policy of the repository: HTML stays fresh, media are frozen.
	# These three rules are the reference: a site that serves the same files
	# locally may copy them, and test that the two do not diverge.
	#
	# The ? prefix sets a default value instead of overwriting: a response coming
	# from a Bun service keeps its own. Without it, a visitor photo served by
	# /photos/<uuid>.jpg went back out with the "public, max-age=31536000,
	# immutable" of @immutable on top of the intended "private, max-age=3600".
	@immutable path *.jpg *.jpeg *.png *.webp *.avif *.svg *.ico *.woff2 *.woff
	header @immutable ?Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"

	@html path *.html /
	header @html ?Cache-Control "public, max-age=0, must-revalidate"

	# A name that carries a content fingerprint, styles.4f3a9c2b.css, designates
	# one single content forever: freezing it for a year cannot go stale, and a
	# modified file arrives all the same since it changes name.
	# A site's own build may fingerprint its assets this way, at deploy time.
	#
	# The rule targets the PATTERN and not the extension, because this block is
	# imported by every site: one that does not fingerprint its files keeps the
	# one-hour rule below, without having anything to declare. It comes before
	# that one for the same reason, the ? setting a default value that the next
	# one will not replace.
	@fingerprinted path_regexp \.[0-9a-f]{8}\.(css|js|mjs)$
	header @fingerprinted ?Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"

	# The stylesheet and the script of a site that does not fingerprint. no-cache
	# does not mean no cache: the browser keeps the file and only asks whether it
	# has changed, to which Caddy answers 304 in a few hundred bytes when nothing
	# has moved. One round trip per file, for two files.
	#
	# That is the price to pay for a stale style to become impossible. The
	# one-hour rule that stood here let a visitor coming back after a release
	# load fresh HTML on top of an old stylesheet, hence a half-drawn page, and
	# nobody saw it from the workstation.
	#
	# The not excludes what @fingerprinted already covers, and it is not decorative:
	# writing the two rules side by side without it let the second win over the
	# first, whatever their order in this file. Two header ? set on the same
	# response are not arbitrated the way one expects; two disjoint matchers
	# are.
	@revalidated {
		path *.css *.js
		not path_regexp \.[0-9a-f]{8}\.(css|js|mjs)$
	}
	header @revalidated ?Cache-Control "no-cache"
}

# The landing. The wildcard does not cover the bare domain: this block therefore
# has its own certificate, for the name of the zone alone. www, for its part, is
# a subdomain like any other and rests on the wildcard.
{$SITESOLIDE_ZONE}, www.{$SITESOLIDE_ZONE} {
	import tls-zone
	import commun

	# The contact form, the landing's only dynamic route. It is served by the
	# service of the landing itself, sitesolide-landing on port 3000, and not by
	# the shared service on port 3001: every site carries its own code, the
	# landing like a client. All the rest of the page is served by the
	# file_server below, without waking Bun.
	#
	# A matcher set on reverse_proxy rather than a handle: the handles of a same
	# block form an exclusive group, which reverse_proxy and file_server do not.
	# See the header of strophe() in api/src/locks.ts.
	@form path /api/*
	reverse_proxy @form 127.0.0.1:3000

	# Nobody signs in on the landing, and the X-Sitesolide-* headers a visitor
	# sends, in any spelling, are taken off before its service reads them, as in
	# every block that reaches an app: see openStanza in bin/cli/portal.ts. The
	# wildcard and the on-demand blocks below need none, they reach no app,
	# only file_server and the preview locks' door page.
	request_header -X-Sitesolide*
	request_header -X_sitesolide*

	root * /srv/sites/{$SITESOLIDE_ZONE}/public
	file_server {
		hide .git .env*
	}

	header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
}

# All client sites on a subdomain, covered by a single wildcard certificate. No
# per-client configuration.
*.{$SITESOLIDE_ZONE} {
	import tls-zone
	import commun
	root * /srv/sites/{$SITESOLIDE_SLUG}/public

	# Neither a repository's history nor a .env is ever served, at any depth,
	# whatever an earlier deployment left in public/: they answer 404. hide
	# takes file names, matched against every component of the path on disk,
	# so .git hides .git/config too. Here and in every file server, the
	# generated blocks' included (FILE_GENERATIONS in bin/cli/fragment.ts);
	# not in (commun), where a matcher would reach the services too.
	file_server {
		hide .git .env*
	}

	# These URLs are previews: the final site will live on the client's domain.
	# Without noindex, the two would compete with each other in Google.
	header X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow"

	# Preview locks, written by the dashboard's gatekeeper from the sitesolide.json files.
	# Each stanza closes a subdomain behind a code, and the header above covers
	# its door page too: it is set before the imported handles, hence on every
	# branch.
	#
	# It is a glob and not a named import because this directory is empty most
	# of the time. A named import of a missing file makes the whole adaptation
	# fail, hence ALL the sites of the machine; a glob with no match is only a
	# warning, just like an imported file that is empty. The directory can thus
	# be created, filled and emptied without risk.
	#
	# The imported file does not need to be read after file_server: in the
	# standard order of directives, handle comes before file_server. The lock is
	# therefore evaluated first, and the file_server above serves only what the
	# handle lets through, that is to say visitors holding the right cookie and
	# the hosts that are not locked.
	import /etc/caddy/locks/*.caddy
}

# Client sites having their own code and their own Bun service.
# Each one places a file here from the projects directory of the workstation.
# These blocks are more specific than the zone wildcard and therefore come first.
import /etc/caddy/sites/*.caddy

# Clients' own domains, which the wildcard does not cover.
#
# This block has no name: it collects everything that no preceding block claims,
# and it is the domaines.map table that decides which directory is served.
# Adding a client is therefore done without touching this file, as for previews.
#
# The certificate goes through HTTP-01, not through the DNS challenge of the
# zone: the Cloudflare token can only modify the zone served, and the client
# often keeps their DNS elsewhere. That is why this block does not import
# tls-zone.
#
# An application client site must not land here: its domain belongs to its own
# file in /etc/caddy/sites/, otherwise Caddy would serve its static files
# without ever waking its service. See docs/concepts.md.
https:// {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}

	import commun

	map {host} {folder} {
		# A domain still certified but removed from the table falls here, and
		# receives a 404 rather than the content of another client.
		default "__unknown"
		import /etc/caddy/domaines.map
	}

	# A site that opens with a code closes on its own domain too: the
	# preview locks recognise the site this block serves by this variable,
	# which no stanza names for an unknown domain. See stanza() in
	# api/src/locks.ts. The same glob as the wildcard block's, with the same
	# reason for being one.
	vars sitesolide_site {folder}
	import /etc/caddy/locks/*.caddy

	# Never an app's folder: an app's names are its own block's, guarded there
	# by its portal or its code. One that reaches this block is one the table
	# still carries and the block in service does not claim, a name taken out
	# of the manifest or one switched on ahead of its deployment, and serving
	# its public/ here would skip the portal. The block is how an app is told
	# from a static site, which has none. A 404 until the app is deployed.
	@app file {
		root /etc/caddy/sites
		try_files {folder}.caddy
	}
	respond @app 404

	root * /srv/sites/{folder}/public
	file_server {
		hide .git .env*
	}
}

# Redirection of client domains arriving in the clear. The names Caddy knows
# already have their automatic redirection; this block covers the domains served
# on demand, which it does not know in advance.
#
# This block occupies the whole of port 80, which might raise fears for the ACME
# challenge. Checked on a client domain: on-demand issuance goes through
# TLS-ALPN-01, on port 443, without ever attempting HTTP-01.
http:// {
	redir https://{host}{uri} permanent
}
